WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.560
Why is the pushdown-to-grammar construction

2
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.740
correct? We must prove that

3
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.940
generated words correspond to empty-stack

4
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.960
computations and that every such

5
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.220
computation can be generated. These

6
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.540
directions use different induction parameters,

7
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.540
so keep the claims separate

8
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.520
rather than hiding everything under

9
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.700
the phrase by induction.

10
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.880
Recall the normalization: one accepting

11
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.940
state, empty stack at acceptance,

12
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.860
and each move pushing or

13
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.220
popping exactly one symbol. A

14
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.640
variable A p q is

15
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.260
intended to generate words taking

16
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.200
p empty to q empty.

17
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.120
Its rules wrap a matched

18
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.040
push and pop, split at

19
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.160
an empty intermediate state, or

20
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.320
generate epsilon from A p

21
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:41.820
p.

22
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.800
First prove the grammar-to-machine direction

23
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.180
by induction on derivation length.

24
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.760
The base case is a

25
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.360
single production A p p

26
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.020
becoming epsilon. A zero-step machine

27
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.740
computation consumes epsilon and leaves

28
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.680
state p and empty stack

29
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.200
unchanged, so it realizes that

30
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.200
generated word.

31
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.080
For a longer derivation, inspect

32
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.680
its first rule. If it

33
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.880
is a matched wrapping rule,

34
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.360
the inner variable has a

35
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.740
shorter derivation of a word

36
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.160
y. By the induction hypothesis,

37
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.400
y takes r empty to

38
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.300
s empty; replay those moves

39
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.840
above the pushed symbol u,

40
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.320
then pop u at the

41
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:19.620
end.

42
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.240
The replay is legitimate because

43
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.600
the inner run never needs

44
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.240
to inspect below its own

45
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.800
stack content. The unchanged lower

46
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.040
symbol simply remains underneath until

47
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.380
the inner run returns to

48
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.240
its baseline. The outer push

49
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.800
and matching pop then produce

50
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.000
exactly the endpoints required by

51
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:37.860
A p q.

52
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.320
If the first rule is

53
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.980
a splitting rule, write the

54
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.440
generated word as y z.

55
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.220
Each child has a shorter

56
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.560
derivation, so the induction hypothesis

57
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.400
gives an empty-stack run from

58
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.740
p to r on y

59
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.340
and one from r to

60
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.760
q on z. Concatenate the

61
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.960
two runs at their common

62
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.160
empty-stack configuration.

63
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.860
Now prove machine-to-grammar by induction

64
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.440
on the number of computation

65
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.040
moves. A zero-move run has

66
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.460
p equal to q and

67
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.120
consumes epsilon. The base production

68
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.300
A p p becoming epsilon

69
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.100
generates exactly that case.

70
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.180
Consider a positive-length empty-to-empty run

71
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.820
that never empties its stack

72
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.360
strictly between its endpoints. Its

73
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.020
first move pushes some symbol

74
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.740
u, and that bottom symbol

75
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.260
cannot be removed until the

76
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.580
last move. Those outer moves

77
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.460
form a matched push-pop pair,

78
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.460
with a shorter inner computation

79
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.160
after stripping off the untouched

80
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:32.800
bottom u.

81
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.820
Apply the induction hypothesis to

82
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.480
that shorter inner run from

83
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.200
r empty to s empty.

84
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.260
It gives a derivation from

85
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.320
A r s of the

86
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.360
consumed inner word y. The

87
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.060
matched wrapping rule then derives

88
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.200
a y b from A

89
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.480
p q, including the possibility

90
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.400
that either outer input label

91
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.260
is epsilon.

92
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.060
If instead the stack becomes

93
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.800
empty at some intermediate state

94
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.560
r, split the run there.

95
00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.680
Both pieces use fewer moves

96
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.300
than the entire run, so

97
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.840
induction gives derivations from A

98
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:03.820
p r and A r

99
00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.480
q. The splitting production joins

100
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.560
their generated words, covering the

101
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.240
other possible shape of computation.

102
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.220
These two cases exhaust positive

103
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.120
normalized empty-to-empty runs. Either the

104
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.720
stack first returns to zero

105
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.160
at the end, or it

106
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.500
has a strictly intermediate zero-height

107
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.120
return. Normalization is what rules

108
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.620
out a no-stack move that

109
00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.340
would evade the push-pop case

110
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:27.740
analysis.

111
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.640
Things to take note of.

112
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.720
Use derivation length in the

113
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.940
first direction and computation moves

114
00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.620
in the second, with their

115
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.280
correct base cases. Explain replay

116
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.780
above an untouched bottom, show

117
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.880
why subproblems are shorter, and

118
00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.720
conclude equality for the start

119
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.200
variable; these proofs are explicit

120
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.580
lecture outcomes, not the excluded

121
00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.980
normal-form appendix.
